We are doing a focused UX/UI simplification run for the Lab Builder app.

Work only in:
/home/administrator/infra-config-portal

Main goal:
Make the app easier for users to understand and operate.

Current problem:
The Provider Status / Lab Builder UI is overwhelming. It shows too much information, too many alarms, too many technical artifacts, and too many competing panels. The user needs a simple, readable, guided workflow that clearly says:
- what is ready
- what is blocked
- what the next action is
- what changed recently
- where advanced details live if needed

This is a UX/product-design run. Focus on screenshots, simplification, readability, layout, and user flow. Do not spend this run on new hardware automation unless needed to support the UI.

Design principles:
1. Progressive disclosure:
   - Show only essential status by default.
   - Hide raw reports, artifacts, JSON paths, command details, and low-level diagnostics behind “Details” or “Advanced”.
2. One primary next action:
   - Every major section should clearly show the next recommended step.
3. Reduce alarm fatigue:
   - Do not show every warning at the top.
   - Group blockers by stage.
   - Show only the highest-priority blocker in the main card.
4. Operator workflow:
   - Organize around the build journey:
     - Lab Profile
     - Cisco Network
     - HPE Server / iLO
     - RAID / Storage
     - ESXi Install
     - NetApp
     - Build Verification
5. Clean visual hierarchy:
   - Fewer cards.
   - More whitespace.
   - Simple status badges.
   - Short text.
   - Consistent headings.
6. Accessibility:
   - Use semantic headings.
   - Clear button text.
   - Visible focus states.
   - Avoid color-only status.
   - Keep contrast readable.
7. Keep power-user detail:
   - Advanced users should still be able to open raw reports/artifacts/commands.

Stage 1: Baseline screenshots
- Start or use the existing dev app.
- Capture current screenshots of:
  - Provider Status page
  - Cisco section
  - HPE/iLO section
  - RAID section
  - ESXi section
  - Build Verification section
- Use Playwright if available.
- If Playwright is missing, install only what is needed or use browser/headless fallback.
- Save screenshots under:
  artifacts/screenshots/ux-baseline-*.png
- Save an audit report:
  artifacts/codex-runs/ux-baseline-audit.md

Stage 2: UX audit
Review the current UI and write:
- top 10 clutter problems
- top 10 confusing labels
- top 10 places where too much detail is shown
- top 10 missing “next action” messages
- sections that should be collapsed by default
- sections that should be combined
- status badges that should be simplified
- data that belongs in Advanced Details

Save:
artifacts/codex-runs/ux-simplification-audit.md

Stage 3: Look up design references
Use internet access if available to review modern dashboard/operator UI patterns, progressive disclosure, accessibility, and readable status pages.
Do not copy any site exactly.
Use the research to make practical design choices for this app.

Save short notes:
artifacts/codex-runs/ux-design-reference-notes.md

Stage 4: Redesign the Provider Status experience
Implement a simpler default UI.

Required changes:
1. Add a “Build Overview” at the top:
   - Overall state
   - Current phase
   - Next action
   - Highest-priority blocker
   - Last successful milestone
2. Replace noisy rows with stage cards:
   - Lab Profile
   - Cisco Network
   - HPE Server
   - RAID / Storage
   - ESXi Install
   - NetApp
   - Build Verification
3. Each stage card should show only:
   - stage status
   - short human-readable message
   - next action
   - one key metric or result
   - “View details” expansion
4. Move raw artifacts, report paths, JSON details, command text, and long lists into details sections.
5. Use calmer language:
   - “Needs attention” instead of giant warning blocks
   - “Not configured yet” instead of scary failure where appropriate
   - “Blocked by prior stage” where that is true
6. Reduce repeated facts:
   - Do not show the same provider mode, report path, and blocker in five places.
7. Add a guided workflow lane:
   - Step 1: Confirm lab profile
   - Step 2: Recover/configure Cisco
   - Step 3: Validate iLO/server
   - Step 4: Validate RAID
   - Step 5: Boot/install ESXi
   - Step 6: Configure NetApp
   - Step 7: Run Build Verification
8. Add an “Advanced diagnostics” drawer or collapsible section for power users.
9. Preserve all existing backend data, but change how it is presented.

Stage 5: Copywriting cleanup
Rewrite UI labels so a new user understands them.

Examples:
- “provider-lab-build-verification” becomes “Run Build Verification”
- “blocked_by_prior_stage” becomes “Waiting on earlier step”
- “stale_config” becomes “Old lab IP detected”
- “operator_action_required” becomes “Needs your action”
- “local-lab-readwrite” becomes “Real Lab Mode”
- “Redfish PATCH accepted” becomes “iLO accepted the storage change”

Save a label map:
artifacts/codex-runs/ux-label-cleanup.md

Stage 6: Implementation
Update frontend files as needed:
- app/frontend/src/App.tsx
- app/frontend/src/types.ts
- app/frontend/src/api.ts
- app/frontend/src/styles.css
- any component files if they exist

If the UI is too monolithic, extract small components:
- BuildOverviewCard
- BuildStageCard
- NextActionPanel
- AdvancedDetails
- StatusPill
- VerificationSummary

Keep it simple. Do not over-engineer.

Stage 7: New screenshots
After changes:
- Run frontend build.
- Start app if needed.
- Capture after screenshots:
  artifacts/screenshots/ux-after-overview.png
  artifacts/screenshots/ux-after-cisco.png
  artifacts/screenshots/ux-after-hpe.png
  artifacts/screenshots/ux-after-esxi.png
  artifacts/screenshots/ux-after-verification.png
- Also create a before/after report:
  artifacts/codex-runs/ux-before-after-report.md

Stage 8: Usability self-check
Check the redesigned UI against this checklist:
- Can a new user tell what to do next in 5 seconds?
- Is there only one primary next action per stage?
- Are advanced details hidden by default?
- Are blockers grouped by stage?
- Are scary warnings reduced to meaningful priority messages?
- Is the lab IP profile obvious?
- Is Cisco’s current blocker obvious?
- Is NetApp clearly marked as not configured yet rather than a mysterious failure?
- Are screenshots readable at normal laptop resolution?
- Does the UI still build?
- Do backend tests still pass where relevant?

Stage 9: Tests and final report
Run:
- frontend build
- relevant backend tests if UI/API types changed
- type checking if available

Save final report:
artifacts/codex-runs/ux-simplification-final-report.md

Expected outcome:
The app should become simpler, calmer, easier to read, and easier to follow. The user should see a guided build flow first, not a wall of diagnostics.

Do not print secrets.
Do not use mock results as substitutes for real-lab status, but fixture/mock UI screenshots are acceptable only for layout testing if the live app cannot load.
